  <div class="web-columns">
    <div>
      <h2>Welcome to the Arcade</h2>
      <p>
        I'm <b>ultimateshades</b>. I collect big-box PC games, map every dungeon on graph
        paper, and argue about the correct order to play the Monkey Island games. This is
        where I write down what happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        My friend <a href="/web/">pants</a> says my CD shelf is "a fire hazard with a
        story." I am choosing to interpret that as a compliment.
      </p>

      <h2>Game of the Month: The Adventures of Monkey Island</h2>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <h3>The Secret of Monkey Island (LucasArts, 1990)</h3>
        <div class="web-post-date">Nov 03, 1998 // 01:17 AM</div>
        <div style="text-align:center;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:13px;background:#000;color:#00ff00;padding:10px;border:2px inset #808080;margin:6px 0">
          ~ THE ADVENTURES OF MONKEY ISLAND ~<br />
          [ GUYBRUSH ] ---------- [ LECHUCK ]<br />
          "You fight like a dairy farmer!"
        </div>
        <p>
          You are Guybrush Threepwood, a mighty pirate. Your goals are simple: become a
          pirate, defeat the ghost pirate LeChuck, and stop saying the word "monkey" in
          front of the wrong people. The SCUMM interface still feels perfect. No parser,
          no guess-the-verb, just click on the world and see what happens.
        </p>
        <p>
          The insult swordfighting is the best puzzle system ever put in a game. Learn the
          comebacks, find the right pirate, and try not to lose to the shopkeeper again.
          My favourites: <i>"You fight like a dairy farmer!"</i> and
          <i>"I've heard you were a contemptible sneak."</i>
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Score: 10/10.</b> Would get distracted by the three-headed monkey again.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="web-panel">
        <h3>Monkey Island 2: LeChuck's Revenge</h3>
        <div class="web-post-date">Oct 29, 1998 // 11:58 PM</div>
        <p>
          Bigger, stranger, and somehow even more obsessed with voodoo. The opening
          cliffhanger is cruel in the best way. I will not spoil it, but if you have not
          played it yet, do not read the walkthrough on the back of the box.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The Adventure Shelf</h2>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <ul style="margin-left:18px">
          <li><b>Day of the Tentacle</b> — time travel, tentacles, and a hamster in a chron-o-john.</li>
          <li><b>Sam &amp; Max Hit the Road</b> — the best road trip in adventure gaming.</li>
          <li><b>Indiana Jones and the Fate of Atlantis</b> — three paths, one fedora.</li>
          <li><b>Grim Fandango</b> — the afterlife has never looked so good in 3D.</li>
          <li><b>Broken Sword: The Shadow of the Templars</b> — Paris, conspiracies, and a very patient goat.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Tips &amp; Tricks</h2>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <p><b>Three Trials:</b> swordfighting, treasure hunting, and thievery. Do them in any order, but save before the swordfight.</p>
        <p><b>Insult swordfighting:</b> write down every comeback you hear. The pirates rotate their lines, so lose a few fights on purpose and collect the responses.</p>
        <p><b>Rubber chicken with a pulley in the middle:</b> yes, it is important. No, I will not tell you where to use it.</p>
      </div>

